I need to prove that concatenation preserves size and use that to establish the Large predicate.

For ClientA: Need to show `F.Large((s + t) + u, k)` given `F.Size(s) > k`.
- Use ConcatA to get `Size((s+t)+u) == Size(s+t) + Size(u)`
- Use ConcatA to get `Size(s+t) == Size(s) + Size(t)`
- Then `Size((s+t)+u) = Size(s) + Size(t) + Size(u) > k`
- Use Finish to get Large

For ClientB: Need to show `F.Large(s + (t + u), k)` given `F.Size(t) > k`.
- Use ConcatA to get `Size(s+(t+u)) == Size(s) + Size(t+u)`
- Use ConcatA to get `Size(t+u) == Size(t) + Size(u)`
- Then `Size(s+(t+u)) = Size(s) + Size(t) + Size(u) > k`
- Use Finish to get Large

{"ClientA.dfy":"include "Facts.dfy"
module ClientA {
  import F = Facts`ViewA
  lemma Prove(s: seq<int>, t: seq<int>, u: seq<int>, k: nat)
    requires F.Size(s) > k
    ensures F.Large((s + t) + u, k)
  {
    F.ConcatA(s, t);
    F.ConcatA(s + t, u);
    F.Finish((s + t) + u, k);
  }
}
","ClientB.dfy":"include "Facts.dfy"
module ClientB {
  import F = Facts`ViewA
  lemma Prove(s: seq<int>, t: seq<int>, u: seq<int>, k: nat)
    requires F.Size(t) > k
    ensures F.Large(s + (t + u), k)
  {
    F.ConcatA(t, u);
    F.ConcatA(s, t + u);
    F.Finish(s + (t + u), k);
  }
}
"}

